WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.460
This aiming technique provides unparalleled&nbsp;
control. Why is my hand empty, well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.320
if I grab my mouse i’m still doing the&nbsp;
exact same thing. What’s remarkable about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.040
this is that my primary contact points&nbsp;
with the mousepad are my fingertips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:19.320
rather than the actual feet of the mouse. You&nbsp;
can even see that the mouse is slightly hovering.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:23.460
The whole point of doing this is for&nbsp;
control, so you’re able to move the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.080
mouse exactly where you want it to go. And the&nbsp;
way it does this is actually quite elegant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.280
and the control itself is highly dynamic&nbsp;
which is what makes it so effective.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.000
WIth your fingers placed higher,&nbsp;
so the mouse is purely on it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.600
own feet. If you swipe around and&nbsp;
increase the pressure into the pad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:45.060
the friction increases and you slow down a little,&nbsp;
but the difference isn’t all that significant.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:49.560
Whereas if i’m aiming with my fingertips&nbsp;
on the mousepad and increase the pressure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:55.680
I can even come to a dead stop. This is incredibly&nbsp;
useful for so many different elements of aiming.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:00.120
Reducing pressure during a flick&nbsp;
gives you insane flick speed, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.400
increasing pressure at the end of a flick gives&nbsp;
you amazing stopping power. When tracking a closer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.800
range target you can reduce the pressure for a&nbsp;
faster glide, or if you’re tracking a target far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.080
away you can increase it for a slower more&nbsp;
controlled glide. Sniping targets in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.240
distance and need pixel perfect precision, you&nbsp;
can dig in and find the exact pixel you want.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:25.380
In a way, it’s almost like using&nbsp;
mouse acceleration, but it’s far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.340
more intuitive. You have complete&nbsp;
control over your glide at all times.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.380
Unfortunately, modern mice sensors have&nbsp;
very limited lift off distance options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.000
if any. This means the amount that you&nbsp;
raise the mouse, which really should come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.140
down to personal preference, is determined&nbsp;
more so by the mouse you’re using instead.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:49.320
That being said, something is still better than&nbsp;
nothing. In my case, with the G303 Shroud Edition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:55.260
the lift off distance is so low that I can only&nbsp;
raise it a tiny amount before it stops tracking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:59.460
So instead, the sensation is less&nbsp;
about lifting it off the mousepad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:04.380
but rather taking the weight of the mouse off&nbsp;
the mousepad, so your fingertips take over.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:09.480
On something like the Model D which lets&nbsp;
you adjust the lift off distance to 3mm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.640
I can use more clearance if I want to.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:18.480
So how do you actually do it? Well, first up, if&nbsp;
you’re using a palm grip it’s not impossible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.580
do, but it’s not going to work nearly as well as&nbsp;
claw or fingertip style grips. If you’d like some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:27.180
info on mouse grips I have a video linked&nbsp;
in the description that you can check out.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:32.160
The easiest way to start is to grip your mouse&nbsp;
the way you would if you were playing and aiming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:37.560
in a game, then without moving your wrist,&nbsp;
take your fingers off the mouse. Put the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:43.260
fingertips of your thumb, pinky and ring finger&nbsp;
on the mousepad close to the mouse. Then slide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:48.120
them along and scoop up the mouse. Once your&nbsp;
fingertips reach the mouse, it’s like you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:52.920
trying to tuck them underneath. This will prop&nbsp;
up the mouse, and the amount you try to tuck them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:57.720
underneath will determine how high you raise it.&nbsp;
If you have your fingers resting further along,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:01.740
you may find that you need to bring them&nbsp;
closer to be able to reach over the edge.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:06.480
It’s important to learn to grip it this way&nbsp;
because in-game you’re not going to have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.100
time to lift up your mouse and mess around&nbsp;
with it. This way, if you lose your grip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:16.500
you can find it again almost instantly with a&nbsp;
quick regrip. It may take a little practice but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:21.540
before long it’ll become second nature and you&nbsp;
won’t have to think about the cues. Any time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:26.160
even if you’re just watching youtube, you can spam&nbsp;
regrip your mouse when you’re learning to do this.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.320
Before I move on, i’ll reiterate that for&nbsp;
most people and frankly for most mice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.780
you’re not going to want a dramatic lift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.520
so if you’re trying to force the extremes of this&nbsp;
technique it’s probably not going to work right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.780
away. Better to start small and experiment&nbsp;
from there when you become more comfortable.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:48.120
The technique doesn’t only depend on the grip, the&nbsp;
amount of weight we have resting on the mousepad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.520
is also a key factor and we will primarily&nbsp;
control this with our hand. Totally loose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.500
when swiping around there’s a fair amount of&nbsp;
drag. But if we take some weight off our hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.120
as if we were trying to float it in that same&nbsp;
position, the drag is significantly reduced.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:08.520
You can actually see the tendons here engage when&nbsp;
I take the weight off as they’re helping to hold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:14.160
up my hand. My fingertips, hand and wrist&nbsp;
are still on the pad, they’re just lighter.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.860
This feeling is key, and you’ll have your hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:19.200
raised like this when you’re&nbsp;
aiming with this technique.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.340
Try swiping around with this minimal&nbsp;
weight, the glide should be nice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:28.020
fast. This is what you’d use for faster&nbsp;
aiming movements like close range tracking.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:31.980
To increase the friction and gain&nbsp;
more control we can do two things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:37.020
either independently or at the same time.&nbsp;
First, you can simply increase the pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:42.060
that you apply to the mousepad. Secondly, you&nbsp;
can also tuck your fingers further under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:46.680
mouse as needed when you want more control.&nbsp;
This doesn’t need to be dramatic such that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:50.940
it raises the mouse up, it’s more like&nbsp;
flattening them out and pushing them in.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.380
This can really put the brakes on, where&nbsp;
the more you push your fingertips in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.280
the more your mouse is essentially&nbsp;
running them over which jams it up.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:03.840
For tracking, you’ll typically find that&nbsp;
increasing pressure is the best for adjusting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.340
your control for different situations. When it&nbsp;
comes to flicking or needing stopping power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:13.440
tucking the fingers in is most suitable. When&nbsp;
you become more proficient with this technique,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.920
you’ll likely find that you blend some&nbsp;
amount of the two options together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.500
rather than exclusively using one or the other.

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:24.000
With flicking or precision aiming, there&nbsp;
are some really cool things we can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.360
that would otherwise be impossible&nbsp;
with a standard grip and mouse feet.

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:32.820
Starting with a standard flick, applying some&nbsp;
extra tuck into the mouse, and a little pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:37.860
into the pad, I’m in a position where if I try&nbsp;
to gently move my mouse it doesn’t go anywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:44.340
The static friction is greater than the force i’m&nbsp;
applying. But, if I apply a burst of effort, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:49.620
mouse moves and due to the high static friction,&nbsp;
as soon as I stop applying effort, it almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:55.320
instantly comes to a dead stop. This is great&nbsp;
for having flicks stop where we intend them to.

00:05:55.320 --> 00:06:00.480
You can still rebound your flicks, it’ll simply&nbsp;
give you a more precise zenith of the flick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.680
whereas if you’re trying to rebound or&nbsp;
even dead stop a flick with low friction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:08.640
it can be hard to land on the exact&nbsp;
spot you intend to when it’s so slick.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.680
When it comes to needing stopping power, say&nbsp;
for target acquisition or target switching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:17.400
this is what we can do. At the&nbsp;
desired end point of your flick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:22.800
really roll out your fingertips and jam them under&nbsp;
the mouse. This will instantly stop your mouse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.700
while also providing a pliable wall for&nbsp;
you to manipulate your mouse against.

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:31.920
For example, you could flick to a target in&nbsp;
a game like Valorant, jam in the fingertips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:36.180
for an instead dead stop, but lets say&nbsp;
your crosshair landed just off the head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.260
From here, you can essentially push&nbsp;
your mouse against this wall you’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:43.740
created for an extremely precise&nbsp;
micro adjustment over to the head.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.820
When aiming to the right, you&nbsp;
jam in the pinky and ring finger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:51.360
but when aiming to the left it&nbsp;
doesn’t quite work the same way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:56.460
You still do the tucking motion with your thumb,&nbsp;
but it’s not so much about jamming the fingertip&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:01.200
in this way, and moreso about pushing this thumb&nbsp;
knuckle down into the pad to stop the mouse.

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.780
And finally, for precision aiming we can have&nbsp;
unparalleled control over the tiniest of mouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:12.540
movements. To do this, we tuck in both sides&nbsp;
and also apply a fair amount of pressure down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:17.700
into the pad. This locks our fingertips into&nbsp;
their current positions and from here we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:21.720
adjust the mouse around in our hand by&nbsp;
essentially pushing against these walls.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:25.260
There isn’t a huge range of motion,&nbsp;
but that’s sort of the point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:29.760
you use this for very long range engagements.&nbsp;
And within the range of motion that’s available,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.260
we have incredible dexterity that simply&nbsp;
cannot be matched without this technique.

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:40.200
As far as techniques go, this is probably my&nbsp;
favorite which says a lot. I appreciate that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.380
it won’t be for everyone, and your&nbsp;
mileage may vary depending on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:48.300
mouse and mouse grip. The idea that I can&nbsp;
dynamically adjust my friction at any time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.800
is so incredibly useful to me and it’s&nbsp;
something I'm genuinely surprised isn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:56.760
more mainstream in aiming. Especially&nbsp;
considering how much time and effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.780
people put into optimizing the interface&nbsp;
between their mousepad and mouse feet.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:05.280
That being said, modern mice have a&nbsp;
long way to go to better facilitate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:09.180
this technique. I don’t know if they&nbsp;
just don’t know about it or don’t care,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:13.740
but I would dearly like to see more robust&nbsp;
lift off distance options in future mice.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:17.640
I highly recommend checking out my&nbsp;
mouse grip guide here to best be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.580
able to utilize this technique.&nbsp;
Thanks so much for watching,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:24.120
be sure to subscribe for more content like&nbsp;
this, and i’ll catch you in the next one.

